Yes. Because the subsets Xi are pairwise disjoint and the partition is data-independent, any pair of neighboring datasets X and X' differs in at most one block, say Xk, while all other Xi (i ≠ k) remain identical. Since each Mi is (εi, δi)-DP on its own block under the same neighboring relation (restricted to Xi), only Mk can incur privacy loss when moving from X to X'; the outputs from the other mechanisms are identically distributed on X and X'. Thus the combined mechanism’s privacy loss on any neighboring pair is bounded by (εk, δk), and taking the worst case over all possible k gives (maxi εi, maxi δi).

This is exactly the parallel composition property stated in [THEOREM 7], applied with D = X and Dj = Xj.

\boxed{\texttt{yes}}
